{
  "version": 1,
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "title": "Q1",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "Explain why the entire read-modify-write sequence in add_comment and add_reply must run inside a single lock, and discuss the tradeoffs between one global lock and per-post locks.",
        "zh-cn": "说明为什么 add_comment 与 add_reply 的整个读-改-写过程必须放在同一把锁内完成，并讨论使用单一全局锁与 per-post 锁之间的权衡。"
      },
      "answer": "Both add_comment and add_reply are read-modify-write sequences: they read some current state (the post's comment map, or a specific comment), compute a new value (the next ID, the new record), and then mutate shared state (bump the counter, insert the new entry). If those steps were not atomic, two concurrent callers could interleave them.\n\nConcretely, with a check-then-act pattern: thread A reads self._next_comment_id as 5, thread B also reads 5 before A has written back 6, and both build a comment with id 5. The ID sequence is no longer unique and one of the two dict entries silently overwrites the other in the store. A second failure mode is torn state: if incrementing the ID counter and inserting the record were in different critical sections, a reader could observe an ID that has been consumed but whose record does not exist yet (or vice versa), so add_reply with that freshly returned comment_id could spuriously raise NotFoundError. A third mode is lost updates on the replies list: read the list, append, write back would drop one of two concurrent replies. Holding one lock across the whole read-modify-write makes the operation linearizable, so every successful commit corresponds to a single well-defined point in time and the IDs, the comment map, and the reply lists always agree with each other. That is also what the ordering requirement depends on: creation order is defined as the order in which operations commit, which is exactly the order in which they acquire the lock, not the order of created_at timestamps (a timestamp taken outside the lock could even be out of order relative to the commit).\n\nTradeoff, single global lock: it is simple to reason about, trivially correct with respect to cross-post invariants, and cheap here because the critical sections are tiny (a dict lookup and an append). Its weakness is serialization: every write on every post contends on the same mutex, so throughput is bounded by one operation at a time and unrelated posts cannot proceed in parallel. As the critical section grows (validation work, serialization, or I/O such as a database write) that single point of contention becomes the bottleneck, and a lock held across I/O is a serious latency hazard.\n\nTradeoff, per-post locks: you keep a lock per post_id (or per post shard), plus a separate small lock for the ID counters (or an atomic counter, or ID allocation moved to a database sequence), so operations on post 10 and post 20 run in parallel. That improves throughput and reduces false sharing, at the cost of more complexity: you need a lock registry that is itself safe to create locks in (a classic double-checked-locking pattern, or a fixed array of striped locks to bound memory), you must avoid deadlocks if any operation ever needs two locks, and you now must reason about cross-post invariants separately. With the ID counter shared across posts, per-post locks do not by themselves make ID generation atomic; the counter still needs its own synchronization, which is exactly the subtlety that a single global lock hides for free.\n\nFor this problem the global lock is the right call: the workload is in-memory, the critical sections are microseconds, and correctness clearly outweighs the marginal parallelism. If profiling later showed contention, the natural next step is striped locks keyed by post_id with an independent atomic ID allocator per shard."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "title": "Q2",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "Explain why the service must return serialized snapshots instead of internal objects, and what could go wrong if get_comments returned internal lists or objects directly, especially under concurrent reads and writes.",
        "zh-cn": "说明为什么服务必须返回序列化快照而不是内部对象，以及如果 get_comments 直接返回内部列表或对象会出现什么问题，尤其是在并发读写场景下。"
      },
      "answer": "The service is the single owner of the truth; the dictionaries it returns are a wire format boundary. If it handed out references to its internal dicts and lists, callers would hold live aliases into the service's state, and every guarantee the service makes about validation, ordering, isolation, and ID uniqueness would become advisory rather than enforced. The test suite checks exactly this: it mutates the returned tree and then re-reads, expecting the original values to survive. Returning internal objects fails that by design.\n\nThe concrete failure modes are numerous. First, silent corruption: a caller doing result[0]['content'] = 'x' or result[0]['replies'].append(...) would rewrite or extend stored state with no validation whatsoever, letting arbitrary or malformed data (wrong types, missing keys) into the store, bypassing every check the service performs on writes. Second, aliasing of mutable children: a comment's replies list is the same object that later writes append to, so a client that captured a snapshot, iterated it, and then had another request land would see the list change under it, or in pathological cases observe a partially constructed reply dict. Third, cross-request leakage and isolation loss: two callers who both received references to the same comment dict could observe each other's edits, and returning the shared post map would let one caller's mutation show up in another post's view, breaking the otherwise guaranteed isolation between posts. Fourth, concurrency hazards like RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration when JSON serialization walks a structure that a concurrent writer is mutating, or torn reads where the encoder sees a reply object with some but not all fields populated. Caching is another trap: if a caller mutates the returned structure and resends or caches it, it corrupts every downstream consumer.\n\nReturning a freshly built snapshot fixes all of these. On each read the service constructs new dicts and new reply lists (a deep copy of the mutable parts) while holding the lock, so the snapshot is internally consistent at one point in time: it never shows a half-written reply and never changes after it is returned. Callers can then mutate the result freely, because it is theirs; the internal store is unreachable from it. It also decouples the API contract from internal representation, so the service can restructure its storage later without breaking clients, and it keeps the returned data types predictable (the same shapes and types produced by add_comment and add_reply), which matters because responses are JSON-serialized. The cost is allocation: building copies is O(comments + replies), which is acceptable because retrieval is expected to be proportional to the size of the result anyway. Note the write path gets the same treatment: add_comment and add_reply also return copies rather than the stored dicts, so a caller that mutates a write response cannot reach back into the store through it."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "title": "Q3",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "Explain how this design would need to evolve for a multi-instance, horizontally scaled deployment, including ID generation and snapshot semantics.",
        "zh-cn": "说明在多实例、水平扩展部署下这套设计需要如何演进，包括 ID 生成与快照语义。"
      },
      "answer": "A single in-process Lock and an in-memory dict only work because there is exactly one process, one address space, and one copy of the state. Once the service runs as multiple instances behind a load balancer, that assumption dies on both axes: mutual exclusion no longer spans the instances, and state is no longer shared. The two things that must be redesigned are ID generation and snapshot/isolation semantics, and they are linked because ordering is currently defined by commit order under the lock.\n\nID generation. The local counters self._next_comment_id and self._next_reply_id are per process, so with N instances every instance would hand out id 1, 2, 3, ... and IDs would collide globally, silently overwriting or confusing records. Options, in rough order of increasing operational weight: (1) a central ID authority, e.g. a database sequence or an atomic increment (Redis INCR), which restores global uniqueness but reintroduces a shared bottleneck and a cross-network hop on every write; (2) pre-allocated ranges or blocks, where each instance leases a chunk of IDs and hands them out locally, amortizing the coordination cost but leaving IDs non-contiguous and creating gaps when an instance leaks or crashes with an unused block; (3) a distributed ID scheme, particularly time-sortable ones such as ULID or a Snowflake-style scheme (timestamp + machine id + per-machine sequence), or a UUIDv7, which gives globally unique, roughly monotonic-by-time identifiers with no coordination at all; (4) a composite key such as (node_id, local_sequence). If the API contract can be loosened from small contiguous integers to opaque unique identifiers, which is by far the cleanest answer, time-sortable IDs also give ordering for free: because the ID encodes creation time and node, sorting by ID reconstructs creation order without a global lock. If IDs must stay integers, the schema should move to 64-bit and the ordering guarantee should be redefined against a monotonic timestamp plus an ID tiebreak (created_at, id) rather than a bare integer. If ordering must be strictly total and contiguous, only a serialized authority can supply it, and it should be designed as an explicit, monitored hot path.\n\nSnapshot semantics. The shared state has to move out of process into a store that multiple instances can coordinate on: a relational database with transactions, Redis, or similar. The role of the in-process lock changes character. It still protects per-instance structures, but the real concurrency control becomes the storage layer: transactions or compare-and-set operations that make each add_comment/add_reply a single atomic commit, plus constraints that enforce the domain rules (a foreign key on comment to post so a missing post fails transactionally, a uniqueness constraint on the reply-to-comment relationship, an index on (post_id, created_at, id) so reads stay proportional to the result set). Read-your-writes must be reconsidered: with a single shared primary that is consistent, but if reads go to replicas, they become eventually consistent and a client that POSTs a comment and immediately GETs the thread may not see it; the fixes are routing reads-for-write-paths to the primary, read-your-own-writes session guarantees (sticky routing or a replication-lag token), or accepting and documenting the latency. Cross-instance cache invalidation, if a cache is added, becomes its own problem: per-post cache keys with explicit invalidation or short TTLs, since otherwise an append on one instance will not be visible on another.\n\nSnapshot also gains a distributed meaning. Today a snapshot is consistent because it is built under a lock in one process. Across instances it must be consistent either because a single transactional query returns the whole thread in one consistent read (a read transaction with a repeatable-read or snapshot isolation level, ordered by created_at/id), or because the aggregation is materialized. If the thread is paginated or the read is spread across shards (say comments sharded by post_id, which is the natural key and keeps a whole thread on one shard and preserves data locality), the API has to expose a cursor or pagination token so callers get a stable, resumable view instead of an unbounded list. In general, the returned dicts remain serialized snapshots, but the guarantee weakens from a single atomic instant in process memory to a consistent read as of some transaction snapshot, from a store that may be eventually consistent for replicas. Idempotency keys on writes also become worthwhile so that retries after a timeout do not create duplicate comments, and the ordering contract should be pinned to a database-provided monotonic sequence rather than to wall-clock time.time(), which is neither monotonic nor synchronized across machines. Finally, the design must decide whether the whole thread is a single shard of truth (simplest: one primary owns a post, matching the per-post lock idea scaled out) or whether cross-post operations exist at all, since per-post ownership is what preserves the isolation guarantee once you have many instances."
    }
  ]
}